WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.440
In spring 2019, Betsy DeVos proposed the
Education Freedom Scholarships, a major

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:14.300
school choice plan that would provide 5
billion in dollar-for-dollar federal tax

00:00:14.309 --> 00:00:17.670
credits for contributions to state
approved scholarship granting

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.840
organizations. Critics pounced, and even
some on the right are skeptical about

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:25.650
federal involvement in private schools.
But this proposal tries to walk

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.090
a tightrope, balancing valid desires to
avoid federal overreach while still

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:34.200
supporting school choice. First, this
approach makes states take charge of

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.350
their own programs.
It leaves Treasury to oversee the

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:41.430
federal tax credit and avoids creating a
new program to be run by the Department

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:46.160
of Education. Second, it can help with the
main challenge facing most state school

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.060
choice programs and tax credit
scholarships in particular: there are low

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:54.220
funding levels. This federal tax credit
could safely encourage another

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.960
source of funds — private donations — to make
scholarships of a reasonable size,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:03.400
large enough to adequately help the mostly
low-income families they serve.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:08.280
Balancing these two interests is a tricky path,
but this proposal could be a good first step.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.600
Do you think the Education Freedom
Scholarships are a legitimate way to

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:17.040
promote school choice? Let us know in our
poll. Also, let us know what other topics

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.460
you'd like our scholars to cover in 60
seconds and be sure to LIKE and

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:25.580
subscribe for more research and videos from AEI.

